  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Leveraging Local Capacity to Strengthen Health Service Delivery  - Human Resource for Health (HRH #3) - Nutrition</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Malawi):  Leveraging Local Capacity to Strengthen Health Service Delivery-Human Resource for Health (HRH#3) - Nutrition 4 Health activity will strengthen the Government of Malawi&apos;s capacity to accelerate long-term and sustained responses to address high burden nutritional disorders such as stunting, wasting, underweight, and micronutrient deficiencies. As such, this activity will strengthen Malawi&apos;s capacity to effectively respond to nutrition and social problems affecting the health of Malawians, thereby accelerating the overall economic development. ..To achieve its objectives, the activity will: i) train and deploy a critical mass of dietitians for all referral and district hospitals in Malawi; ii) establish an association of dietitians in Malawi to govern the dietetic profession and practice; iii) update the food composition database and tables containing no less than six hundred foods that are commonly consumed in Malawi; iv) strengthen research, outreach, and teaching capacity in nutrition, dietetics, and food science at Lilongwe University of Agriculture and Natural Sciences; v) train staff at Masters and PhD levels in Nutrition and Dietetics, and undertake and publish ethically-sound research; vi) develop a curriculum in nutrition that will respond to health needs during and after climate related emergencies. USAID&apos;s support to Lilongwe University of Agriculture and Natural Sciences enhances local institutional capacity to ensure program continuity after the activity closes out. ..The activity supports the State-USAID Joint Regional Strategy Goal 4.3 to sustainably reduce hunger, malnutrition, and poverty in sub-Saharan Africa. The activity also supports the Integrated Country Strategy Mission Objective 3.2 Malawi improves access to and quality of health services so that more of its citizens gain and retain full health and can contribute productively at home, in the community, and at work....</narrative>
  </description>
